        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Size a Commercial Dehumidifier for a Large Space
          </h2>

          <p>
            The square footage on a commercial listing (2,100, 2,300, 3,000 square feet) is a marketing estimate, just as it is on a household unit. It assumes a typical ceiling and a moderate moisture load, and it tells you nothing about the doors that open to the loading dock, the concrete slab that wicks groundwater or the propane heater that adds water vapor to a shop every time it runs. Moisture load, not floor area, decides how many pints you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            A more useful starting point is volume and dampness. Multiply length by width by ceiling height for cubic feet. A dry, closed storage space that only needs holding at 50% might be handled by one 85 AHAM pint unit per 15,000 to 20,000 cubic feet; a damp basement or a building with regular door traffic may need one per 8,000 to 10,000. These are rules of thumb, not engineering, and a large or unusual building deserves a proper load calculation from an HVAC contractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, two smaller units often beat one big one. A dehumidifier dries the air that passes through it, and a portable throws air perhaps 20 to 30 feet. In a long warehouse or a basement shaped like an L, two Storm Pros at opposite ends will hold more even humidity than one Storm Elite in the middle, cost about the same, and give you a backup when one needs service. The Elite earns its place in a big open room where one machine and one drain are simpler.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest sizing tool is a $15 hygrometer. Put two or three around the space for a week, note the readings morning and afternoon, and watch what happens when the doors are open. If the space sits at 60% to 65%, one well-placed unit will usually pull it to 50%. If it sits above 70% or the walls sweat, fix the water source first. Our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a> lists accurate cheap ones, and the <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement sizing guide</a> works through the household-scale arithmetic.
          </p>

