        <div id="amp-draw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 120V Amp Draw: Will It Trip Your Breaker?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every unit in this guide plugs into an ordinary 120 volt outlet, but they do not all draw the same current, and on a job site or in an older building that difference decides whether the breaker holds. Listings put the AlorAir Storm Pro at roughly 5.5 to 6 amps, the Sentinel HD90 at about 5.6, the Abestorm Hurricane 800 at around 5.3 by our arithmetic from its 607 watt rating, the BlueDri BD-76 at 7.3, the BD-130P at 7.5, the Santa Fe Impact155 at 8.0 and the Storm Elite at 9.0.
          </p>

          <p>
            A standard household circuit is 15 amps, and the electrical code treats a load that runs for three hours or more as continuous, which should not exceed 80% of the breaker rating: 12 amps on a 15 amp circuit. A dehumidifier running all day in a warehouse is exactly that kind of load. So a 9 amp Storm Elite leaves about 3 amps for anything else on the circuit, which is one work light, not a shop vac and a heater. Two Storm Pros on the same circuit, at about 11 to 12 amps combined, are right at the limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compressor start-up matters too. A dehumidifier draws a brief surge when the compressor kicks on, higher than its running current, and that surge is what usually trips a breaker that is already loaded with tools. On a job site, give each big dehumidifier its own circuit or its own leg of a temporary power panel. If you must use an extension cord, use a short, heavy one (12 gauge for anything over 50 feet) rather than a thin household cord, which drops voltage and makes the compressor work harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running cost follows the same numbers. A 680 watt Storm Pro running 20 hours a day uses about 13.6 kWh, roughly $2.30 a day at 17 cents per kWh; a 1,035 watt Storm Elite uses about 20.7 kWh, roughly $3.50. That is our arithmetic from listed wattage, not a measured bill. Efficiency differs more than you might expect: the ENERGY STAR Sentinel HD90 lists about 2.69 liters per kWh. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> covers the method.
          </p>

